The era of the human encyclopedia is officially dead. ❌
The professionals who will thrive tomorrow aren’t the ones who memorize every line of syntax or hoard static knowledge. They are the ones driven by relentless curiosity and an obsession with how things actually work.
But honestly? It took me a while to realize this.
The past few months, I’ve been feeling completely overwhelmed. I’ve been hopping between multiple projects and trying to learn several new technologies all at once.
I tried to memorize everything. I failed.
I kept finding myself going back to my old notes or asking AI to explain how a specific function worked. It was incredibly frustrating. I actually started to worry that I was falling behind.
Then, I stopped and asked myself a few brutal questions:
• Am I getting my job done? Yes.
• Am I meeting my deadlines? Yes.
• Can I break down problems, plan the steps, and prioritize? Yes.
• Can I debug the system when it breaks? Yes.
That’s when the lightbulb went off. 💡
The way we were brought up to learn just doesn't work anymore. Things are moving too fast. If you try to memorize it all, you will burn out. Especially in the age of AI.
I realized I don't need a flawless memory. I need execution. I just need to understand the core principles.
AI has become my car, and I am the driver. 🏎️
I use it to put pieces together, write scripts, generate DAX formulas, and translate human language into computer language. I am still the one building, creating, and debugging. But AI is my leverage. It’s my superpower.
It’s like having Master Yoda or a world-class senior engineer sitting right next to me. 🧠
I can ask it to criticize my work, give me examples of how to make it look more professional, and give me instant feedback. I am focusing entirely on hands-on learning—doing, breaking, and testing. I am growing faster because of it.
If I don't use a specific tool for a few weeks, I might forget the exact syntax. And that is completely okay. Within two minutes, AI can refresh my memory because I already understand the underlying logic.
So, stop being angry at your brain for forgetting all of the DAX formulas, toolsets, or lines of code. 👋
Don’t bother trying to memorize the universe. Start prioritizing understanding over memorization.
Your value isn't what you hold in your head. It's what you can build with the tools in front of you.
My memory might not be perfect. But give me a problem, give me a little time, and give me AI—and I will figure it out and build it. Every single time.
What about you? Are you still trying to memorize everything, or have you made the shift to curiosity and leverage? 👇
